+   "root_cause":"Symptom: a sale with a very large negative amount (e.g. -1000000000000000000000000000) gets a 500 and is logged at Error. Cause: SellPolicyRequest.cs:62 calculates `amount * 100` twice inside IValidatableObject.Validate. The only guard before it is `amount > MaxAmount` (MaxAmount is set at :24), which bounds positive amounts only. Any decimal below roughly decimal.MinValue/100 (about -7.92e26) still binds under strict JSON, because it is within decimal range. It passes the `>` test, and then the multiplication throws OverflowException. Nothing catches it in the validation path, so it reaches UnexpectedExceptionHandler.cs:26-28 (LogError and 500). The comment at SellPolicyRequest.cs:61, 'Checking the limit first keeps * 100 from overflowing', is true only for positive values. This finding has the same cause as I2 (R1). They stay separate because they cite different rules: an unhandled exception any caller can trigger (API4) here, and the lost SELL-7 422 in I2.",
